{
 "id": "2026-barwicka-wind",
 "name": "Wind Turbines in Rural Landscapes: Visual Assessment (rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Barwicka, S., Barwicki, P., & Milecka, M. (2026). Visual assessment of wind turbine impacts on rural landscapes in Poland: a model-based SBE study considering distance from residential areas. Land 15:435. This is the paper's Google Forms questionnaire (Supplementary Materials). Respondent characteristics come first (age group, gender, education, place of residence). Participants then rate computer visualizations (SketchUp models rendered in Lumion) of seven rural landscape types: large-scale arable farming, agricultural–forest, mosaic agricultural, agricultural–settlement, meadow–pasture, orchard and horticultural, and mountain agricultural. Each type is shown as a reference view without wind turbines and with turbines 500, 750 and 1000 m from the residential buildings, one image at a time in random order. The question is \"How do you rate the visual attractiveness of the presented landscape?\" on a 1–10 scale (1 = least, 10 = most visually attractive). Three control questions follow: knowledge of wind energy, the landscape type considered most attractive, and professional contact with agriculture, landscape architecture or forestry. Analysis as in the paper: Scenic Beauty Estimation (ratings standardised to z-scores per respondent, averaged per visualization), two-factor ANOVA of landscape type × turbine distance, and a linear trend over distance. Original result (104 respondents): the reference views were rated highest, the mountain agricultural reference highest of all (Z = 1.20); ratings increased with setback distance; the large-scale arable farming landscape at 500 m was rated lowest (Z = −0.886); open landscapes were more sensitive to turbines than structurally complex ones.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) 27 visualizations instead of 28. In the published supplementary questionnaire the mountain 1000 m image is a byte-identical repeat of the 750 m image, so that variant is not available and is left out. (2) The agricultural–settlement 1000 m image is also missing from the supplement (it repeats the 750 m image), so it is taken from the paper's Figure 4F, with the panel letter removed (884 × 496 px). (3) The variant of each image was identified by comparing turbine size against the reference views and Figure 4.\n\nVisualizations: the paper's Supplementary Materials and Figure 4 (CC BY 4.0), used as published with credit to the authors; the template is removed if the authors object.",
